okay in this lesson we're going to talk  about scientific notation something you  definitely want to know for the act so  let's just kind of go over this real  quick so scientific notation is when you  write a really large number or a really  small number in a compact form usually  with a 10 to a power so the idea is you  want just one digit a single digit  between 1 and 9 okay in front of the  decimal point and then the remainder of  the significant digits after the decimal  point and then raised to 10 to the power  okay so an example would be like say you  had three million eight hundred thousand  right now the decimal points right here  we wanna move it to right there so we  just have the three just one digit in  front of the decimal point so we have to  move it one two three four five six  places so that's 3.8  times 10 to the sixth so when you see  this positive exponent here that tells  you to move the decimal six places to  the right 10 to the sixth is uh  a million right so basically that's 3.8  times a million and you're moving that  to the right now this is a really small  number here we want that decimal point  to end up right there so that we just  have one  single digit okay between one and nine  in front of the decimal point so we're  going to be moving this one two three  four five six seven places so that's  going to be 5.73  times 10 to the negative seventh okay  the negative tells us that when you see  a number like this is very small and you  would be moving this decimal point seven  places to the left  now when you work with scientific  numbers if you're multiplying or  dividing for example when you multiply  you'd multiply these numbers here in  front so 3 times 5 is 15  and then you'd multiply the power of  tens so 10 squared times 10 cubed is 10  to the fifth because remember when you  multiply and you have the same base  you're adding the exponents right  but when you look at this number it's  really not in scientific notation  because you see the decimal points right  there so we have to move it one place to  the left that's making it smaller by a  power of 10. we have to counterbalance  that by adding 1  to the power of 10 here so that we don't  change the overall value of the number  so this is actually 1.5 times 10 to the  sixth power  okay now this one we're dividing we're  going to divide these two numbers 4  divided by 2 is 2  and then 10 to the 8th okay divided by  10 to the first when you divide you  subtract the exponent so that's 10 to  the 7th this one is already in  scientific notation it's just 2.0 times  10 to the 7th okay let's look at a  typical example here so see if you can  do this one on your own and then you  know go ahead we'll uh go through it  together so if a spaceship can travel  8.0 times 10 to the 8th meters per  second and if it wants to travel 4 times  10 to the 20th meters how long will it  take to arrive in seconds  okay so see if you can do that on your  own  so the way we would do this is you would  take the distance okay which is 4 times  10 to the 20th okay meters divided by  the time which is  i'm sorry by the speed which is eight  point zero times ten to the eighth  meters per second  and you can see here four divided by  eight gives us  one half which is 0.5 and then 10 to the  20th divided by 10 to the eighth that's  going to be 10 to the 12th because we're  subtracting and then this is going to be  seconds but we want to convert this into  a scientific number by moving this  decimal point so now that it's 5.0 we  made this number larger when you move  the decimal point to the right so to  counter balance that we're gonna have to  subtract one  from the exponent there and make this  ten to the eleventh seconds now the way  i did this problem is you know the  formula distance equals rate times time  right  so basically we're trying to solve for  the the time so we divided by the rate  okay that's the speed so by taking the  distance okay which is this guy here  divided by the rate which is this guy  here we were able to solve for the time  so another good formula to know distance  equals rate times time
 

